WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.134 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-I feel such gratitude

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.602 line:92%
any time any Broadway performe

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:05.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
uses their off day on a Monday
to spend time with me.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:08.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:09.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Seth, right?
-Yeah.

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:10.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Is this you?

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:11.544 size:81% position:41%
Am I on the Seth show?
-Well, and this is --

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.047 align:start size:94% position:6%
your show, you've been doing
this since November,

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:17.384
which I really tip my cap.
You have yet to miss a show.

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:18.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-That's really amazing.

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:21.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Eight shows a week.
We're in our seventh month

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.790 size:88% position:44% line:92%
I got five months to go.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's Atticus Finch.
-Yeah.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.794
-It's Atticus Finch.
And it's the role of a lifetime.

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:31.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we got the reviews.
We got the critics.

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:34.367 size:84% position:42%
We got the -- We got --
The people are coming.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You got a Tony nomination
-That happened.

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:38.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Congratulations.
-That happened.

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.408 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:42.509 line:92%
This is -- This is not --

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is not the gig
where you call your agent

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:45.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
and go,
"Get me something better."

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right, right.
-"Get me something better.

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:50.483
So, this is pretty good.
-And again, Atticus Finch,

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
needless to say,
this is a character --

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:54.421 size:84% position:42%
"beloved" almost seems
like an understatement.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:57.857
Did you find it daunting to take
on this role in this play?

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, well, yeah.
Yes, and no.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, Gregory Peck
did the movie,

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:04.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he won an Oscar for it
-Yeah.

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:06.533 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-And people revere him.

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was --
They only made the one movie

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.638
There were no other plays.
There were no other movies

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:16.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so people think of
Atticus Finch as Gregory Peck.

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:20.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the people
who love the book are --

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:23.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are three kinds of peopl
who come to the show.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
The people
who absolutely love the book

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they almost clutch it
to their chest

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:31.925
while they're watching us,
going, "Don't ruin this."

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:36.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then there are people who
absolutely love Gregory Peck

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:38.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're not him.
-Right.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:39.599 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:42.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And then,
there are the teachers.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:45.338 line:92%
There are the teachers who -

00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:49.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
Months before even rehearsal
started, I had a guy.

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:50.143 line:92%
I was at something, and he goes,

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:53.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You know, I've taught the boo
for 35 years."

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.247 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like it was, you know,
a challenge.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:56.983 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.983 --> 00:02:00.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I go, "Well, you're gonn
hate the nude scenes."

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:03.490 line:92%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.924 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Well, so...

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:08.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it's -- it's --
Look, we've got them.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.498 size:75% position:38%
We come at the story
in a different way.

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:14.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
And especially now,
in today's America,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
with race and everything
that's going on

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.806
with Trump and all that,
it's more timely than ever

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:22.075 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. I mean, it's incredible
that you take this work

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:25.345 align:start size:94% position:6%
from the '30s and --
I mean, it's sadly resonant.

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:26.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
You would hope
that there would be --

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:29.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
what was addressed then
would no longer be relevant.

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:32.085
And obviously, you knew that
with the undertaking of it

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that something that was,
obviously, not even

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
the back of your mind
but the front of your mind

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
as you were putting
this production together?

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:40.527
-You knew that "Mockingbird"
had the chance to say something.

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:44.397
and so often, the American
theater is kind of forgotten

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:47.867
I think 2% of the populous
has ever been to a play.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:51.671
That includes Aunt Martha in a
four-hour version of "Oklahoma!"

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the community theater.
-Right, right, right.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:57.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, 2%.
So, when the American theate

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:00.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
can say something,
can have a voice,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:03.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you see what happens
every night,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.652 line:92%
the 1,400 people that sit there,

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:08.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's not like watching
the movie or reading the book,

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.990 size:88% position:44%
which you can put down
or you can put on pause.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're in that room,
and they've shut the doors

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:18.398 size:81% position:41%
And when Tom Robinson,
who is 100% innocent,

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:21.467 line:92%
is found guilty, they feel it.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:22.769 line:92%
It's a different experience,

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
and only the theater
can do that.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:26.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
And with Aaron Sorkin
and Bart Sher,

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:28.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
everybody in the cast that
pulled this thing off

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:32.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
night after night after night,
we still change people.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:34.214 align:start size:94% position:6%
We can still --
We send people out the doo

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:37.650
thinking differently, and that's
all you can ask of any play.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, it's an incredible --
it's an incredible undertaking

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the fact that you succee
in that is amazing.

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:45.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it must be so differen
than selling out

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:49.329
the Shubert Theatre in you
early days here in New York.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:52.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
What were some of your
early shows like?

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:54.867 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Well, um...um...

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:56.169 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:58.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had some success.
I was off Broadway.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:02.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
-Making 125 bucks a week.

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:04.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
Proud to get it.
Glad to get it.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:06.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
Occasionally, though,
you did the play

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
that no one came to see.
-Sure.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:10.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Even off Broadway.
-Yeah.

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:12.418
What does "no one" mean?
-What does "no one" mean?

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:13.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're in a hundred --
you're in --

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:14.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, like this.
-Yeah.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:17.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
-An audience about
however many people this is.

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:19.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there
are seven people out there

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:21.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's a lot of empty seats.
-Seven people.

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:24.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And you are a cast of eight

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.499 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:27.767 line:92%
So you outnumber the audience.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:28.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So at least you know,
worst comes to --

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:31.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And every single time
that happens --

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:33.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's happened
more than once.

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:35.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
Every time it happens,
you go to the stage manager,

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:38.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
you go, "You know,
I think there's a union rule

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
where if we outnumber
the audience,

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
we don't have to do the show."
-Sure, sure.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And he pretends
he's looking it up and goes,

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:45.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
"No, there isn't a rule.
You got to go on."

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:46.586 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
So, you go on,

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:48.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
you play to the seven people
you get to intermission,

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:51.324
you get back out, and thre
of the people have left.

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:53.293 line:92%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:57.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah.
-Now I -- genuine question

00:04:57.397 --> 00:04:57.997 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Do you think --

00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:00.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think they leave
because they don't like it

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:02.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
or do you think
it's just too much pressur

00:05:02.035 --> 00:05:04.404 size:84% position:42%
to be that few people?
-There's some pressure.

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:07.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
Especially when you go
for the laughs.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, my God!
You're still trying to sell it

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:12.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
with eight people
in the audience?

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:15.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That was a good one.
You missed it.

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:19.819
That, plus, I think, they're
just going, "Why are we here

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
This must be really bad."
-Yeah.

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:22.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-This must -- What?

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:26.626
-No -- I mean, yeah, you think
like, "What did we get wrong?"

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:29.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"We screwed up.
We're at the wrong show."

